    53	Every row still needs the embedding into the original kernel coordinates and the real kernel-limit interpretation. The roadmap admits **attaching maps**, and the attachment evidence explicitly leaves real curve-selection and variation along boundary orbits open. Base-orbit transitivity cannot supply missing kernel transport: the split boost has no lift. [`roadmap:335–337`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/notes/rank_locus_two_dimension_gap.md:335), [`attachment README:97–108`](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/README.md:97), [`kernel program:23`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/design/a5-kernel-bundles-nullity8-program.md:23)
    54	
    55	The concrete tooling priorities are these.
    56	
    57	| Priority | Tooling to build or extend | Expected payoff | Falsifier for the payoff |
    58	|---|---|---|---|
    59	| **1** | **A degree-by-degree exact fiber driver** joining Singular generation, normal-form extraction, FLINT rank/kernel calculation, relation verification, and cumulative relation files | Replaces manually assembled quadratic/cubic scripts with restartable, comparable degree receipts. The existing templates already implement the main algebra; the extractor already computes a left kernel. [`a2_body.sing:3–12`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/a2_body.sing:3), [`relations_nf.py:32–51`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/relations_nf.py:32) | Any mismatch with the retained counts, a recovered relation failing exact membership, or a reordered/omitted product slot invalidates the driver. |
    60	| **2** | **A completion-certificate layer** supporting prime-support density, independently proved Hilbert bounds, and structural presentations | Can close a fiber without repeating a large saturation. It must distinguish support equality from graded-ring equality. The consultation already identifies density in an irreducible support as an alternative. [`consult:141`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:141) | A certificate that merely reproduces \(K_2\)’s own invariants, or checks several unchanged degrees, supplies no independent lower bound. |
    61	| **3** | **A second-prime and Gaussian-rational reconstruction harness** | Detects exceptional-prime behavior and turns modular candidates into characteristic-zero identities after verification. The producer already distinguishes \(\mathbb Q(i)\) from the two split embeddings. [`producer:119–123`](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:119), [`634–650`](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:634) | Prime-dependent generator counts, leading ideals, ranks, or failed rational membership stop promotion of the result. |
    62	| **4** | **A matrix-export and certified-rank interface**, initially FLINT, optionally LinBox | Makes large coefficient problems measurable and interchangeable. Sparse Macaulay upper/lower bounds can certify an individual degree when standard-basis construction becomes excessive. [`consult:427–442`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:427) | Matrix construction or polynomial reduction dominates runtime, or sparse rank loses to FLINT after conversion and certificate costs. |
    63	
    64	The degree driver should retain, for each pair, field, and degree: input hashes; original generator identities; ordered target monomials; cumulative certified relations; source standard basis; normal forms including zero slots; matrix dimensions and nonzero count; rank and relation witnesses; stage timings; and memory/termination records. Multiplication should reduce modulo \(J\) after each factor, following the successful cubic inputs. [`X cubic input:12–20`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s1/input.sing:12)
    65	
    66	The driver should report a finite degree as **certified**, while reporting full-fiber completeness as a separate status. Its mathematical basis is
    67	\[
    68	h_d=\dim_k\operatorname{span}\operatorname{NF}_{J+\mathfrak m P_d}(P_d).
    69	\]
    70	The quotient is annihilated by \(\mathfrak m\), which explains why global ideal membership recovers the local graded piece. [`consult:35–57`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:35)
    71	
    72	For faster completion, I would pursue three arguments in order:
    73	
    74	1. **Prime support plus an independent dimension witness.** For X, retain the prime defining the reduced quadratic support and construct a family of genuine limiting kernel planes with three-dimensional image. Containment and equal dimension inside that irreducible support would prove reduced-support equality over the established field. The family needs a density or function-field argument; a finite collection of arcs supplies a lower sample only. The existing X support calculation makes this route unusually concrete. [`support log:4–5`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pC_xm_reduce.log:4)
    75	
    76	2. **A structural presentation with a proved Hilbert series.** E8-bar→VB deserves the first recognition experiment. Its reported values \(10,34,80,155\), dimension three, and degree seven are consistent with
    77	   \[
    78	   H(z)=\frac{1+6z}{(1-z)^4}.
    79	   \]
    80	   That arithmetic is a hypothesis generator, not a computed full Hilbert series. Search for a small determinantal or scroll presentation and verify ideal equality. A \(2\times7\) matrix has 21 two-by-two minors, matching the quadratic-relation count; failure to find and verify such a presentation ends that particular hypothesis. The input measurements are the recorded VB quadratic model. [`decision-record.md:157–159`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:157)
    81	
    82	3. **An independently bounded full Hilbert function.** If a proven relation-type bound limits every minimal fiber relation to degree \(D\), method A through \(D\) closes the algebra. Alternatively, a surjection from a candidate algebra to \(F(I)\), together with an independently established equal Hilbert series, proves equality degree by degree. Regularity of the candidate \(k[y]/K_2\) supplies neither condition; that shortcut was correctly rejected. [`decision-record.md:24`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:24)
    83	
    84	A Cohen–Macaulay or regular-sequence argument must prove something about the relevant presentation. The generator counts already exceed the germ dimensions, so the supplied ideals \(I\) cannot themselves be regular sequences. A useful alternative is an unmixed candidate algebra plus independently established equal dimension and multiplicity: a nonzero kernel would then contribute top-dimensional multiplicity.
    85	
    86	**X illustrates why the hypotheses matter.** Its quadratic scheme and radical have the same dimension and degree, while their Hilbert functions differ: \(15,51,122,\ldots\) versus \(12,45,112,\ldots\). Thus dimension and degree alone already fail to distinguish two different graded structures in the retained example. Reduced-support completion would leave those nilpotent directions unresolved. [`rad_xm.log:1–14`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/review-checks/claude/rad_xm.log:1)
    87	
    88	The modular harness must regenerate coefficients from the original Gaussian-rational source. Replacing `32009` in an already specialized numeric input cannot recover another reduction. It should evaluate both images of \(i\), normalize bases before matching coefficients, perform Chinese-remainder/rational reconstruction, and verify the lifted identities over \(\mathbb Q(i)\). Agreement at two primes is supporting evidence; the characteristic-zero membership and completeness argument provide the certificate. Preserve the existing retained records as predecessors.
    89	
