   101	
   102	I would **defer GPU rank infrastructure**. The completed logs show thousands of seconds in standard-basis construction before matrix rank is available. GPU acceleration cannot remove that polynomial bottleneck. First export a matrix whose exact rank dominates end-to-end cost; then compare a finite-field implementation against FLINT/LinBox, including transfer and certification. Floating-point rank and characteristic-two substitution would change the evidential problem. [`E8 timing:1–4`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/e8_a2/out.log:1), [`W8 timing:1–4`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/w8_a2/out.log:1), [`consult:440,470`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:440)
   103	
   104	I would also avoid rebuilding the whole algebra stack, repeating the old unrestricted graph elimination, launching extra sign-equivalent jobs, or treating `DegreeLimit` as protection for Macaulay2 `reesIdeal`: the installed implementation explicitly says that option has no effect. [`ReesAlgebra.m2:113–122`](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:113)
   105	
   106	Placement should follow the measured mechanism and an aggregate memory budget. The host incident involved several concurrent workloads, a terminal scope using about 64 GB plus 27 GB of swap, and an uncapped Singular job. The later W8 failure occurred inside its own 6-GiB cgroup, a different boundary. The record supports controlling concurrency; it does not identify one process as the sole cause of the terminal-scope kill. [`decision-record.md:125–136`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:125)
   107	
   108	The following are **proposed budgets**, not completion predictions. The listed substrate capacities come from the consultation resource inventory. [`codex-consult-brief.md:19–25`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-brief.md:19)
   109	
   110	| Substrate | Jobs to place there | Memory and time budget |
   111	|---|---|---|
   112	| **Host** | Driver development; symmetry and relation checks; small target-ideal decomposition; VB completion experiments; short X/Iso structural probes | One heavy job at **8–16 GiB**, optionally one **4-GiB** small job. Keep the aggregate admitted algebra budget at **24 GiB** initially. Give exploratory certificates **15–60 minutes**; give VB A/B attempts **1–3 hours** before reviewing the result. |
   113	| **Kaggle CPU** | Second-prime degree replays; longer X/Iso A calculations; E8/W8 cubic calculations whose measured footprint fits | **24-GiB working budget**, one heavy process, **10-hour solver deadline** within the twelve-hour session. Reserve time and memory for export and collection. Use container limits and a watchdog where delegated cgroups are unavailable. |
   114	| **Azure VM, one per region** | Large full-fiber saturation, E8-bar→N−, and matrix constructions that exceed Kaggle’s measured envelope | One heavy job per VM. Start at **64 GiB**, increasing toward **72 GiB** only after measuring operating-system headroom. Use **6–10-hour attempts**, stage exports, and an independent VM deallocation deadline. Run the E8-bar and W8 completion jobs sequentially. |
   115	| **GitHub Actions** | Deterministic relation verification, provenance/schema checks, symmetry checks, small replay controls; scheduled bounded independent checks | **8–12 GiB** job budget. Keep ordinary PR additions short; use scheduled/manual jobs of **30 minutes–2 hours** for larger verification. The cited runner envelope is 16 GiB and six hours, leaving little room for discovery computations near that ceiling. |
   116	
   117	The current VM 2 case allocates `78G` per job; VM 1 records two `38G` allocations. Those are recorded choices, not safe defaults to copy: the documents alternate “84 GB” and “84 GiB.” A future launcher should read `MemTotal`, budget in bytes, and reserve explicit headroom. [`VM case:17–25`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-cases/vm2-fiber-b.md:17), [`decision-record.md:143–146`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:143)
   118	
   119	Every heavy launch should have its own memory cgroup, zero swap allowance, an active wall deadline, and recorded peak/events. The admission check must account for all simultaneously reachable allocations. A queue should release work after observing the actual predecessor unit exit. Deadline extensions should replace or reconfigure an active timer; the supervisor must remain runnable. The repository already requires memory/time/flag review and distinguishes resident-memory limits from address-space limits. [`compute-flags.md:51–65`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:51), [`179–196`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:179)
   120	
   121	The next concrete milestone should be **one complete, independently checked fiber receipt**, preferably E8-bar→VB if its small target presentation yields to structural recognition. That receipt should connect full algebra, embedded components, characteristic-zero verification, and the remaining real attachment obligation. It would validate the machinery needed to turn the other five rows from quadratic candidates into roadmap-item-6 evidence.
